 Plan: env.clipboardHistory Extension API
  
 Context

 The goal is to add vscode.env.clipboardHistory as a new proposed extension API that lets extensions read the system clipboard history (a list of recently-copied text items). This follows the same lifecycle as every other
 proposed VS Code API: a type declaration in vscode.proposed.*.d.ts → an IPC shape registered in extHost.protocol.ts → an ExtHost-side thin wrapper → a main-thread implementation backed by a platform service → wiring in
 extHost.api.impl.ts → a checkProposedApiEnabled guard → opt-in via enabledApiProposals in package.json.

 Note: This repo (/Users/asridhar/work/Sourcegraph/code/cmp/vscode) is a partial checkout. The VS Code core src/ tree is absent; only extensions/copilot/ and build tooling are present. The key implementation pattern was
 reverse-engineered from the fixture file at:

 extensions/copilot/src/extension/prompts/node/test/fixtures/extHost.api.impl.ts

 All src/ paths below refer to the upstream VS Code core — add them to a full checkout of microsoft/vscode.

 ---
 How the Existing Extension API Pipeline Works

 Understanding the full pipeline before adding anything new:

 Layer 1 — Public type declaration (src/vscode-dts/)

 vscode.d.ts declares the stable API surface. New proposed additions go in a separate file:

 src/vscode-dts/vscode.proposed.<feature>.d.ts

 These use declare module 'vscode' { ... } to augment the module without redefining it. Example: vscode.proposed.envShellEvent.d.ts adds env.onDidChangeShell.

 Layer 2 — IPC channel shapes (src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHost.protocol.ts)

 This single file is the contract between the Extension Host process (sandbox) and the Main/Renderer process. It defines two namespaces of typed channel identifiers:

 - MainContext — identifiers for main-thread services that the extHost can call into
 - ExtHostContext — identifiers for extHost actors that the main thread can call into

 Each entry pairs a string key (for serialization) with a TypeScript interface (for type-safe proxying):

 // excerpt showing the pattern
 export const MainContext = {
   MainThreadClipboard: createMainId<MainThreadClipboardShape>('MainThreadClipboard'),
   MainThreadCommands: createMainId<MainThreadCommandsShape>('MainThreadCommands'),
   // ...
 };

 export interface MainThreadClipboardShape extends IDisposable {
   $readText(): Promise<string>;
   $writeText(value: string): Promise<void>;
   // --- NEW: add here ---
   $readClipboardHistory(): Promise<{ value: string; timestamp: number }[]>;
 }

 Methods on these interfaces are prefixed $ by convention (marks them as RPC-destined).

 Layer 3 — ExtHost-side actor (src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostClipboard.ts)

 The ExtHost class holds a proxy obtained from rpcProtocol.getProxy(MainContext.MainThreadClipboard) and wraps it in a developer-friendly API:

 export class ExtHostClipboard {
   readonly value: vscode.Clipboard;
   constructor(mainContext: IMainContext) {
     const proxy = mainContext.getProxy(MainContext.MainThreadClipboard);
     this.value = Object.freeze({
       readText: () => proxy.$readText(),
       writeText: (value) => proxy.$writeText(value),
     });
   }
 }

 ExtHostClipboard is not registered in ExtHostContext (it never receives calls from the main thread), so it is created as a plain non-addressable instance (lines 206–207 of the fixture).

 Layer 4 — Main-thread implementation (src/vs/workbench/api/browser/mainThreadClipboard.ts)

 The main-thread class is decorated with @extHostNamedCustomer(MainContext.MainThreadClipboard) so the framework auto-registers it on startup. It injects the platform IClipboardService:

 @extHostNamedCustomer(MainContext.MainThreadClipboard)
 export class MainThreadClipboard implements MainThreadClipboardShape {
   constructor(
     _extHostContext: IExtHostContext,
     @IClipboardService private readonly _clipboardService: IClipboardService
   ) {}

   dispose(): void {}
   $readText(): Promise<string> { return this._clipboardService.readText(); }
   $writeText(value: string): Promise<void> { return this._clipboardService.writeText(value); }
 }

 Layer 5 — API factory wiring (src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHost.api.impl.ts)

 The createApiFactoryAndRegisterActors factory is where all extHost actors are instantiated and returned to extensions as the vscode namespace object. For clipboard (non-addressable, lines 206–207 + 324):

 // instantiation (outside the per-extension factory closure)
 const extHostClipboard = new ExtHostClipboard(rpcProtocol);

 // wiring into env namespace (inside the per-extension factory closure)
 const env: typeof vscode.env = {
   // ...
   get clipboard(): vscode.Clipboard { return extHostClipboard.value; },
 };

 Proposed APIs are gated immediately before returning:

 get onDidChangeShell() {
   checkProposedApiEnabled(extension, 'envShellEvent');
   return extHostTerminalService.onDidChangeShell;
 },

 Layer 6 — Extension opt-in (package.json + vscode-api.d.ts)

 An extension must declare each proposal it uses in two places:

 package.json:
 "enabledApiProposals": ["clipboardHistory"]

 src/extension/vscode-api.d.ts (copilot extension, or any other extension):
 /// <reference path="../../../../src/vscode-dts/vscode.proposed.clipboardHistory.d.ts" />

 ---
 Step-by-Step Plan: Adding env.clipboardHistory

 Step 1 — Define the proposed API type

 File: src/vscode-dts/vscode.proposed.clipboardHistory.d.ts (NEW)

 declare module 'vscode' {
   /**
    * A single item in the system clipboard history.
    */
   export interface ClipboardHistoryItem {
     /** The text content of this clipboard entry. */
     readonly value: string;
     /** ISO timestamp when this entry was copied. */
     readonly timestamp: number;
   }

   export namespace env {
     /**
      * Read the system clipboard history, newest first.
      * Requires the `clipboardHistory` proposed API to be enabled.
      *
      * @returns A thenable that resolves to an ordered list of recent clipboard entries.
      */
     export function readClipboardHistory(): Thenable<ClipboardHistoryItem[]>;
   }
 }

 Step 2 — Add the IPC method to the clipboard shape

 File: src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHost.protocol.ts

 Find MainThreadClipboardShape and add one method:

 export interface MainThreadClipboardShape extends IDisposable {
   $readText(): Promise<string>;
   $writeText(value: string): Promise<void>;
   $readClipboardHistory(): Promise<Array<{ value: string; timestamp: number }>>;  // ADD
 }

 No new ExtHostContext or MainContext entry is needed — this rides the existing MainContext.MainThreadClipboard channel.

 Step 3 — Extend the ExtHost clipboard wrapper

 File: src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostClipboard.ts

 Add a readHistory() method on the ExtHostClipboard class and expose it. The existing value: vscode.Clipboard object cannot grow (it is frozen and typed by vscode.d.ts), so expose readHistory directly on the class:

 export class ExtHostClipboard {
   readonly value: vscode.Clipboard;
   private readonly _proxy: MainThreadClipboardShape;

   constructor(mainContext: IMainContext) {
     this._proxy = mainContext.getProxy(MainContext.MainThreadClipboard);
     this.value = Object.freeze({
       readText: () => this._proxy.$readText(),
       writeText: (value) => this._proxy.$writeText(value),
     });
   }

   readHistory(): Promise<vscode.ClipboardHistoryItem[]> {
     return this._proxy.$readClipboardHistory();
   }
 }

 Step 4 — Implement the main-thread side

 File: src/vs/workbench/api/browser/mainThreadClipboard.ts

 Add $readClipboardHistory() to the MainThreadClipboard class. The platform IClipboardService interface will need a corresponding readHistory?() method added (or check if it already provides it via the ClipboardItem API). A
 simple fallback for platforms that don't support history is to return the current text as a single-item array:

 async $readClipboardHistory(): Promise<Array<{ value: string; timestamp: number }>> {
   // IClipboardService.readHistory() must be implemented per platform.
   // Fallback: single-item array from current clipboard text.
   if (typeof this._clipboardService.readHistory === 'function') {
     return this._clipboardService.readHistory();
   }
   const text = await this._clipboardService.readText();
   return text ? [{ value: text, timestamp: Date.now() }] : [];
 }

 Also extend IClipboardService in src/vs/platform/clipboard/common/clipboardService.ts:

 export interface IClipboardService {
   readText(type?: string): Promise<string>;
   writeText(value: string, type?: string): Promise<void>;
   readHistory?(): Promise<Array<{ value: string; timestamp: number }>>;  // ADD optional
 }

 Platform implementations (src/vs/platform/clipboard/browser/clipboardService.ts, electron-sandbox/clipboardService.ts) can implement it using the native Clipboard History API or Electron's clipboard module.

 Step 5 — Wire into the env namespace

 File: src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHost.api.impl.ts

 The extHostClipboard instance is already created as a non-addressable object (line 207 of fixture). Add readClipboardHistory to the env namespace object:

 // namespace: env
 const env: typeof vscode.env = {
   // ... existing members ...
   get clipboard(): vscode.Clipboard { return extHostClipboard.value; },

   // ADD:
   readClipboardHistory() {
     checkProposedApiEnabled(extension, 'clipboardHistory');
     return extHostClipboard.readHistory();
   },
 };

 Step 6 — Register the proposal name in the validator

 File: src/vs/workbench/services/extensions/common/extensions.ts

 Find the allApiProposals or equivalent allowlist and add 'clipboardHistory'. This is what checkProposedApiEnabled validates against:

 export const allApiProposals = Object.freeze({
   // ... existing entries ...
   clipboardHistory: 'Enable access to system clipboard history.',
 });

